<% content_for :title, "Character Counter" %>
<div class="mb-8">
  <p class="mb-2 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-primary">Forms</p>
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight mb-3">Character Counter</h1>
  <p class="text-muted-foreground">
    A Rails model says <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">validates :bio, length: { maximum: 500 }</code>,
    and this tells the person typing before they submit. The limit is soft — nothing stops typing or pasting, and the
    server still decides — but the count is exactly what <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">validates length:</code>
    will count, rendered as <%= link_to "Field", field_path, class: "text-primary underline" %>'s own help text.
  </p>
</div>

<%# Preview %>
<h2 class="text-base font-semibold mb-3">Preview</h2>
<div id="character-counter-preview" class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-background p-12 mb-8">
  <div class="w-80">
    <%= render Ui::FieldComponent.new(name: "demo[bio]") do |field| %>
      <% field.with_label { "Bio" } %>
      <% field.with_description { "A couple of sentences about you." } %>
      <% field.with_control(Ui::TextareaComponent, counter: true, limit: 60, rows: 3) %>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 class="text-base font-semibold mb-3">Usage</h2>
<div class="rounded-lg overflow-hidden border border-border mb-8">
  <div class="flex items-center justify-between bg-muted px-4 py-2">
    <span class="text-xs text-muted-foreground">ERB</span>
    <button data-copy-target="#character-counter-usage" class="text-xs text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors">Copy</button>
  </div>
  <pre class="bg-card px-5 py-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm leading-relaxed" tabindex="0"><code id="character-counter-usage" class="text-card-foreground"><%= h(example_character_counter_usage) %></code></pre>
</div>

<%# Over the limit %>
<h2 class="text-base font-semibold mb-3">Past the limit</h2>
<p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-3">
  Nothing stops typing or pasting — <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">maxlength</code> is
  still yours to set if you want that. Over the limit the count turns destructive
  (<code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">data-over="true"</code>) and nothing else changes: no
  <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">aria-invalid</code>, no error, no change to the field's
  invalid state. Validity stays the server's.
</p>
<div id="character-counter-over-preview" class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-background p-12 mb-8">
  <div class="w-80">
    <%= render Ui::FieldComponent.new(model: DemoNote.new(body: "This note is already longer than its twenty character limit."),
                                      attribute: :body, control_id: "character_counter_over") do |field| %>
      <% field.with_label { "Short note" } %>
      <% field.with_control(Ui::TextareaComponent, counter: true, limit: 20, rows: 2) %>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</div>

<%# In a form %>
<h2 class="text-base font-semibold mb-3">In a form</h2>
<p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-3">
  The counter never validates — a blank note here is still rejected by the model's own presence
  validator, the same way it would be without a counter at all. Field's help-text-and-error swap
  is what shows the failure; the count rides along inside it, tracked even while hidden, and comes
  back showing what is currently typed once the field is valid again.
</p>
<div id="character-counter-form-preview" class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-background p-12 mb-8">
  <%= render "docs/character_counter_swap_demo", note: DemoNote.new, submission: 0 %>
</div>

<h2 class="text-base font-semibold mb-3">Options</h2>
<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-border mb-8">
  <table class="w-full text-sm">
    <thead class="bg-muted">
      <tr>
        <th class="text-start px-4 py-3 text-xs font-semibold text-muted-foreground uppercase tracking-wider">Name</th>
        <th class="text-start px-4 py-3 text-xs font-semibold text-muted-foreground uppercase tracking-wider">Type</th>
        <th class="text-start px-4 py-3 text-xs font-semibold text-muted-foreground uppercase tracking-wider">Default</th>
        <th class="text-start px-4 py-3 text-xs font-semibold text-muted-foreground uppercase tracking-wider">Description</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody class="divide-y divide-border">
      <% [
        ["counter",  "Boolean", "false", "Ui::TextareaComponent(counter:). Consumed, never forwarded to the element. Needs limit:, and a Field to render its count in."],
        ["limit",    "Integer", "nil",   "The soft limit the count is measured against. Needs counter: true. Never sets maxlength."],
      ].each do |name, type, default, desc| %>
        <tr class="hover:bg-muted/50">
          <td class="px-4 py-3 font-mono text-xs text-primary"><%= name %></td>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 text-muted-foreground"><%= type %></td>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 font-mono text-xs text-muted-foreground"><%= default %></td>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 text-muted-foreground"><%= desc %></td>
        </tr>
      <% end %>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<h2 class="text-base font-semibold mb-3">Accessibility</h2>
<ul class="text-sm text-muted-foreground space-y-2 mb-8 list-disc ps-5">
  <li>The count is the last child of the description part, so it joins the control's computed
    <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">aria-describedby</code> after the help text —
    read on focus, not as a live region.</li>
  <li>A polite <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">role="status"</code> region, visually
    hidden, announces exactly three moments: ten percent of the limit remaining, exceeding the limit, and coming
    back to it or under. Typing inside a band announces nothing.</li>
  <li>Past the limit, nothing changes but the count's own colour: never
    <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">aria-invalid</code>, never an error. A validation
    failure is a different signal from a soft limit.</li>
</ul>
